// A parallel block followed by more statements -- the join.
//
// MoRDor's own test, for #81. Everything after the block used to be discarded:
// interpret_statements_open's Threads branch returned the cross product of the
// thread structures and never recursed into the rest of the list, so `r1 := x`
// and `y := r1` below produced no event and bound no register. An assertion
// naming r1 was then decided against a free variable, which is why this file's
// forbid is the assertion it is -- allow (r1 = 0) passed either way.
//
// 999 is never written to x, so no execution can end with r1 holding it. Fails
// outright if the continuation goes back to being dropped.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  r2 := y
} ||| {
  r3 := x
};
r1 := x;
y := r1;
%% forbid (r1 = 999) []
